Apple will have to contend with a patent lawsuit in India over its NameDrop contact-sharing feature, as a U.S. startup is seeking 15 billion rupees (approximately &#36;162 million) in damages and restrictions on sales of devices that include the feature, reports <em><a href="https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/20/startup-sues-apple-in-india-over-tap-to-share-contacts-feature/">TechCrunch</a></em>.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2023/12/ios-17-namedrop-pink.jpg" alt="" width="1600" height="900" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-924979" /><br/>
The company behind the lawsuit is GG Technologies, which is responsible for the contact-sharing app <a href="https://staytouch.com/">StayTouch</a>. It filed the case in the Bombay High Court in October. According to court documents seen by <em>TechCrunch</em>, the company alleges NameDrop infringes its Indian patent covering technology that allows nearby phones to exchange contact details without manual pairing.<br/>
<br/>
The startup is asking the court to prevent Apple from selling or importing iPhones and Apple Watches that support the NameDrop feature in India. The case had not yet been listed for a hearing. <br/>
<br/>
GG Technologies launched StayTouch in 2019, and received its Indian patent in July 2024 after applying in 2021. The lawsuit apparently followed unsuccessful mediation between the companies, according to the startup, which also sued Apple in the U.S. in April over related patent infringement claims.<br/>
<br/>
In August, the International Trade Commission (ITC) opened an investigation in the U.S. into Apple's alleged patent infringement, following the complaint from GG Technologies. The startup wants to see an order that could block imports of infringing Apple devices into the country, as well as restrictions on their sale.<br/>
<br/>
Apple announced <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2023/06/05/namedrop-apple-ios-17/">NameDrop</a> back in June 2023 with the release of iOS 17. The feature lets users exchange contact information by bringing compatible devices close together, although sharing <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/how-to/ios-share-contact-details-with-namedrop/">requires user confirmation</a>. It can be easy to forget that the feature exists, and it's not really clear how many people use it on a regular basis.<div class="linkback">Tags: <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/india/">India</a>, <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/techcrunch-com/">TechCrunch</a></div><br/>This article, &quot;<a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/11/23/iphone-sales-ban-namedrop-feature/">iPhone Sales Ban in India Sought by Startup Over NameDrop Feature</a>&quot; first appeared on <a href="https://www.macrumors.com">MacRumors.com</a><br/><br/><a href="https://forums.macrumors.com/threads/iphone-sales-ban-in-india-sought-by-startup-over-namedrop-feature.2495030/">Discuss this article</a> in our forums<br/><br/>